  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations in Delaware are $223M for FY2026 to date. Award count in Delaware is 3,422 and average award size is $65.1K. Obligations fell 39.5% year over year. Historical obligations by fiscal year show variation: FY21 was $321M, FY22 was $81.3M, FY23 was $325M, FY24 was $337M, FY25 was $368M, and FY26 is $223M. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $103M obligated in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $43.4M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $19.6M in the same period. Stonewin International, LLC is the largest vendor with $24M in obligations, followed by Caesar Rodney School District with $15M and Aquia Nava II LLC with $14.6M. Other notable industry concentrations include Other Computer Related Services at $24.9M and Petroleum Refineries at $24M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did obligations fall year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 39.5% year over year. Fiscal-year comparisons show FY25 obligations were $368M and FY26 obligations are $223M, indicating a lower total in the current fiscal-year-to-date period. The Department of Defense remains the largest buyer with $103M, which accounts for a meaningful share of current-year spending. Award count is 3,422 and average award size is $65.1K, suggesting many smaller awards contributed to the total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate Delaware contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $103M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Health and Human Services follow with $43.4M and $19.6M respectively. Other Computer Related Services is the largest industry category at $24.9M, with Petroleum Refineries close behind at $24M. Stonewin International, LLC is the top vendor with $24M in obligations, and Caesar Rodney School District is the next largest vendor at $15M."
      }
    ]
  },
